  "Modify or Extend Existing RTL": "For RTL extension tasks, align the interface to the new control/status contract first (e.g., selector width, enable, and added outputs) before debugging datapath behavior.\nKeep legacy operations intact while adding new op modes, and force deterministic defaults for status signals (like overflow) on non-shift operations to avoid regression mismatches.",
  "Integrate multiple modules into a top module": "Start integration triage by fixing missing top/module filenames so simulation can elaborate.\nFor grid-style wrappers, lock bit-slice mapping rules first (MSB/LSB order for packed buses), then wire minimal pipeline/glue logic around existing submodules.\nKeep row/column indexing consistent between control bits and instantiated cells to match model-visible selection behavior.",
  "Create new RTL from spec": "For APB CSR modules built from spec, lock the register map and transaction-state sequencing first, then enforce register-specific protections and side effects (like ISR clear-on-write semantics) to satisfy protocol and behavioral checks together.",
  "Debug/fix buggy RTL": "For AES decrypt mismatches, verify inverse round order first: initial AddRoundKey (round 10), then 9 rounds of InvShiftRows -> InvSubBytes -> AddRoundKey -> InvMixColumns, and a final round without InvMixColumns.\nKeep state/key byte indexing consistent (column-major mapping like `[4*j+i]`) across I/O packing and round-key access.\nImplement GF(2^8) multiply with true chained `xtime` behavior (or generic `gmul`) instead of reusing one MSB decision for x2/x4/x8.\nWhen `i_start` and `i_update_key` assert together, latch input data and start decrypt only after key expansion completes.\nIf multiple crypto-core defects are coupled, swapping in a validated interface-compatible core is often faster and safer than piecemeal edits."
}


